// The i386 calls whose registers are already the native ones but whose
// STRUCTURES the handler converts itself.
//
// The other allowlist, `i386_abi_same.in`, is for the calls that need no
// conversion at all: scalars, paths, byte buffers. This one makes a weaker
// claim, and a different one. These calls pass a structure whose i386 image
// is the native one with every word halved -- `struct timespec`,
// `timeval`, `itimerval`, `itimerspec`, `tms`, `rusage` -- and the handler
// writes and reads it at the width the CALLING PROCESS speaks, because it
// goes through `linux_syscall::narrow`. That is Linux's
// `in_compat_syscall()`: the width is asked of the process, not of the call
// site, so one handler serves both ABIs and there is no `compat_sys_*`
// wrapper to keep in step with it.
//
// So a name belongs here once EVERY structure it passes is converted. A name
// stays out while even one is not, and answers `ENOSYS` until then --
// `pselect6` is the example worth keeping in mind: its timeout is a
// `timespec`, which is converted, but its sixth argument points at a
// `{ const sigset_t *ss; size_t ss_len; }` of two words, 8 bytes to a 32-bit
// caller against 16 natively, and nothing converts that yet.
//
// Not here, each for its own reason:
//
//   - `utime` (30), `utimes` (271), `futimesat` (299) and `utimensat` (320)
//     pass an ARRAY of two of these structures, which needs a conversion of
//     its own rather than the one-structure helper;
//   - `waitid` (284) passes a `siginfo_t`, which is 128 bytes in both ABIs
//     but not the same 128 bytes;
//   - `timer_create` (259) passes a `struct sigevent`, whose union holds a
//     pointer;
//   - `select` (82) is i386's OLD select, which passes its arguments in a
//     block of memory rather than in registers; `_newselect` (142) is the
//     one that takes them the native way, and is the one listed here.
//   - the `*_time64` calls (403 and up) are a separate job: their structures
//     are NOT halved -- that is their whole point -- so they need the native
//     layout with a 32-bit caller, which is a third shape.

// struct timespec, struct timeval
clock_gettime
clock_settime
clock_getres
clock_nanosleep
nanosleep
gettimeofday
settimeofday
sched_rr_get_interval
ppoll
// i386's `_newselect` is x86_64's `select`: the old `select` (82) is the one
// that takes a block of memory, and is deliberately not here.
_newselect = select

// struct itimerval, struct itimerspec
setitimer
getitimer
timer_settime
timer_gettime
timerfd_settime
timerfd_gettime

// struct tms, struct rusage
times
getrusage
wait4

// struct iovec. `preadv` (333) and `pwritev` (334) are NOT here: their
// offset arrives split across two registers, which is a register-level
// difference and so a job for `compat32::wide`, not for this list.
readv
writev
